WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.180
A monkey watches a cloud of dots, some moving together and the rest at random.

2
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.500
A neuron in its parietal cortex fires faster and faster.

3
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:17.360
On average, near a certain level, the monkey moves its eyes to answer.

4
00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.800
Is that neuron making the decision?

5
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:25.380
Silence the area it sits in, and in one study the monkey decides just as well.

6
00:00:26.183 --> 00:00:28.963
Coherence is the percentage of dots that move together.

7
00:00:29.563 --> 00:00:32.583
Neurons in area MT are tuned to the direction of motion.

8
00:00:33.143 --> 00:00:37.123
In monkeys, each MT neuron was tested on a task matched to it.

9
00:00:37.603 --> 00:00:41.763
Most of the neurons studied discriminated motion about as well as the monkey, or

10
00:00:41.763 --> 00:00:47.103
better. Weak current into a small cluster of MT neurons with one preferred direction

11
00:00:47.103 --> 00:00:49.723
biased the monkeys' choices toward it.

12
00:00:50.203 --> 00:00:53.863
So MT supplies evidence, and its activity can sway the choice.

13
00:00:54.733 --> 00:01:00.533
The lateral intraparietal area, or LIP, holds neurons that fire before eye movements

14
00:01:00.533 --> 00:01:01.933
into their response field.

15
00:01:02.633 --> 00:01:06.113
Roitman and Shadlen let rhesus monkeys answer whenever they were ready.

16
00:01:06.853 --> 00:01:12.473
Stronger motion gave faster, more accurate choices, and LIP firing rose more

17
00:01:12.473 --> 00:01:17.233
steeply. It reached a similar level shortly before the eye movement, which the

18
00:01:17.233 --> 00:01:18.493
authors read as a threshold.

19
00:01:18.493 --> 00:01:24.213
These ramps are averages across trials, and whether single trials ramp or step is

20
00:01:24.213 --> 00:01:25.213
debated.

21
00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:29.467
A drift -to -bound model adds up evidence until the total hits a bound.

22
00:01:29.987 --> 00:01:34.767
In this invented example, with no noise, the rate starts at 30 spikes per second.

23
00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:40.927
It climbs to 70 after a latency of 200 milliseconds, and the eye moves 70

24
00:01:40.927 --> 00:01:41.947
milliseconds later.

25
00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:48.927
At 51 .2 % coherence the slope is 400, and the reaction time is 370 milliseconds.

26
00:01:48.927 --> 00:01:55.087
At 12 .8%, it is 670, and at 3 .2%, it is 1 ,870.

27
00:01:56.367 --> 00:02:01.167
Correlation shows what activity goes with a choice, but only a manipulation can test

28
00:02:01.167 --> 00:02:02.527
whether the choice needs it.

29
00:02:03.347 --> 00:02:07.847
Cats, Yates, Pillow and Huk silenced each area in turn in rhesus monkeys.

30
00:02:08.507 --> 00:02:12.347
They used muscimol, a drug that activates GABA -A receptors.

31
00:02:13.227 --> 00:02:15.887
Silencing MT impaired motion discrimination.

32
00:02:16.547 --> 00:02:21.347
Silencing LIP produced no measurable impairment of motion decisions in the tasks

33
00:02:21.347 --> 00:02:22.347
tested.

34
00:02:22.667 --> 00:02:26.807
A missed injection could explain a null result, so the same monkeys did a free

35
00:02:26.807 --> 00:02:28.747
-choice task, with no right answer.

36
00:02:29.507 --> 00:02:35.107
After LIP inactivation, they chose targets in the affected part of space less often.

37
00:02:35.727 --> 00:02:38.527
Their eye movements to that side landed less accurately.

38
00:02:39.247 --> 00:02:43.947
The drug had acted, yet no deficit appeared, though other areas may compensate.

39
00:02:43.947 --> 00:02:49.327
Keep this rule: a neuron that tracks a choice is not always needed for it.
